narrative:
## Building a Math Toolkit

Math functions are the purest form of functions: take a number, transform it, return the result. Let's build several.

### Square and Cube

```xiom
fn square(n: Int) -> Int {
  return n * n;
}

fn cube(n: Int) -> Int {
  return n * n * n;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(square(5));  // 25
  io.println(cube(3));    // 27
  return 0;
}
```

### Even and Odd Checking

Functions that return `Bool` are perfect for yes/no questions:

```xiom
fn is_even(n: Int) -> Bool {
  return n % 2 == 0;
}

fn is_odd(n: Int) -> Bool {
  return n % 2 != 0;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(is_even(10));  // true
  io.println(is_odd(7));    // true
  return 0;
}
```

### Putting Them Together

```xiom
fn describe_number(n: Int) {
  io.println("Number: ");
  io.println(n);
  io.println("Squared: ");
  io.println(square(n));
  io.println("Cubed: ");
  io.println(cube(n));
  if is_even(n) {
    io.println("It's even!");
  } else {
    io.println("It's odd!");
  }
}

fn main() -> Int {
  describe_number(7);
  return 0;
}
```

Each function does ONE thing well. `describe_number` uses them all — that's the power of composition!

